In this lesson we'll look at how real numbers behave when you add, subtract, multiply, or divide them. The big idea is closure: whenever you do any of those operations on real numbers, the result is still a real number, with one obvious rule that you can never divide by zero.

Let's break down what happens with different combinations. When you add or multiply two rational numbers, you always get another rational number, like one half plus one third gives five sixths. The interesting part is when irrationals enter the picture. Adding two irrationals is a coin flip, sometimes you land on a rational number, like root two plus negative root two gives zero, but other times you stay irrational, like root two plus root three. And here's a handy rule to remember, a rational plus an irrational is always irrational, because a nice clean fraction can never cancel out that messy infinite tail.

You'll also lean on knowing the common irrationals. If your calculator spits out a long decimal and you spot root two hiding in there, that's a great sanity check. And don't forget Pythagoras. Any right triangle with whole number sides that don't form a perfect square will give you an irrational hypotenuse, like the classic one-one-root-two triangle, or one-two-root five. Now let's see it in action.